// MAINTAINER NOTE, not documentation -- two properties of the shape below are
// load-bearing and easy to "simplify" into a defect:
//
//   * THE GUARD IS NESTED, not one expression. `compile has(V) &&
//     target_cfg_pkg::X` -- the LRM's own Example275 -- is a hard error in
//     pssparser, which evaluates both operands of a compile-time binary
//     expression eagerly. Short-circuiting is normative (§8.4.4) but it governs
//     EVALUATION, not name resolution. `!compile has(V) || ...` is the same bet
//     mirrored; also avoid it.
//
//   * THE DEFAULT IS TRUE, i.e. the richer wait. Defaulting to false would drop
//     `wake` and notify_irq() and still elaborate -- a hang or livelock in
//     simulation rather than a diagnostic. Defaulting to true fails at the
//     missing runtime primitive instead, loudly and with a location.
//
// There is no `package target_cfg_pkg { }` stub in this tree and none is
// needed: `compile has` naming an undeclared package evaluates to false, so an
// unconfigured build reaches the `else`. A real provider comes from pssc's
// target prelude, ahead of every file here, and is obliged to declare every
// contract constant -- so a misspelling is a compile error, not a silent
// fallback to the default.

// This model's build configuration, derived from the execution target's.
//
// The adapter, and the only file that mentions ``target_cfg_pkg`` or
// ``compile has``. Everything downstream reads the two constants below. The
// full contract is ``docs/target-cfg-contract.md``.
//
// **HAS_EVENT_WAIT** -- can a caller on this target wait for an EVENT? It
// selects the wait PRIMITIVE and nothing else:
//
// ``true``
//     ``wb_dma_ch_c.wake`` and ``wb_dma_c::notify_irq()`` exist, and
//     ``wait_hint()`` suspends on the channel.
//
// ``false``
//     Neither exists, and ``wait_hint()`` spins with ``yield``.
//
// It does NOT select the operation surface: ``wait_completion()``, the three
// end-to-end operations and their scenario actions are present on BOTH
// profiles, written against ``wait_hint()``, so a firmware caller gets the same
// API a UVM test gets. It replaced ``HAS_BLOCKING`` ("can the runtime suspend a
// thread?"), which conflated that with whether there is an event to wait on at
// all. Only the latter is a real capability -- every target can spin.
//
// **HAS_SOLVER** -- gates the SCENARIO layer, the actions in
// ``wb_dma_ch_c/actions/blocking_ops_a.pss``. Those have ``rand`` fields, and a
// target with no solver in the image has nothing to populate them with. It is
// separate from ``HAS_EVENT_WAIT`` so that a target with a solver and no event
// source, or the reverse, is expressible. Both current profiles answer both
// questions the same way (``op-model-sv`` true/true, ``op-model-c``
// false/false).
package wb_dma_cfg_pkg {

    compile if (compile has(target_cfg_pkg::TARGET_CFG_VERSION)) {
        static const bool HAS_EVENT_WAIT = target_cfg_pkg::HAVE_EVENT_WAIT;
        static const bool HAS_SOLVER     = target_cfg_pkg::HAVE_RUNTIME_SOLVER;
    } else {
        static const bool HAS_EVENT_WAIT = true;
        static const bool HAS_SOLVER     = true;
    }

}
